    <h1 style="color:#0f1f3d;">Truck Batteries for the African Market</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Africa is a core JIS market — Japanese, Korean and Chinese heavy trucks dominate most of the
      continent, running JIS N150/N200 batteries — with European vehicles taking DIN/EN in North
      Africa and South Africa. DINWEYS supplies both families, built for heat, dust and rough terrain.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:20px 0;padding:16px 20px;background:#f4f7fb;border-left:4px solid #f5a623;border-radius:6px;">
      <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.15rem;">Direct Answer</h2>
      <p style="margin:0;">Africa's heavy-truck fleet runs a Japanese base — Hino, Isuzu, Mitsubishi Fuso and UD Trucks — alongside a fast-growing Chinese segment (Sinotruk HOWO, FAW, Shacman) and a European long-haul/mining segment (Mercedes-Benz Actros, Volvo FH, Scania, MAN). The dominant battery group is JIS N150 (145G51) and N200 (190H52), with DIN88 (58827) and DIN100 (60038) serving European vehicles in North Africa and South Africa. Heat, dust and rough-road vibration are the defining duty stress, and imports clear through country-specific conformity schemes — SONCAP in Nigeria, PVoC in Kenya, SABS in South Africa — with Durban, Mombasa, Lagos and Tema as key entry ports.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Market Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Africa's heavy-duty truck market is worth roughly <strong>USD 2.85&nbsp;billion (2026)</strong>,
      growing at a 4.35% CAGR to about USD 3.52&nbsp;billion by 2031, with Daimler Trucks
      (Mercedes-Benz), Volvo Trucks, Scania AB, MAN Truck &amp; Bus and Sinotruk (CNHTC) cited as the
      major operators
      (<a href="https://www.mordorintelligence.com/industry-reports/africa-heavy-duty-truck-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mordor Intelligence</a>).
      The wider African battery market is worth about <strong>USD 2.81&nbsp;billion (2026)</strong>,
      expanding at an 11.55% CAGR
      (<a href="https://www.mordorintelligence.com/industry-reports/africa-battery-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mordor Intelligence</a>).
      Industry tracking of the region's best-selling tractor trucks names Isuzu, Sinotruk HOWO, UD
      Trucks, Mercedes-Benz Actros, Volvo FH and FAW among the leaders
      (<a href="https://www.semitrailernews.com/share/africa-best-selling-tractor-trucks-2025.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SemiTrailerNews</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      Japanese brands were the historical default from Lagos to Nairobi, but Chinese trucks have
      captured a growing share over the past decade
      (<a href="https://www.hebeicar.com/used-chinese-trucks-vs-japanese-trucks-africa-2026-comparison/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hebeicar</a>).
      The precise installed-fleet split by brand is not published in a single official source, so
      treat the mix above as structure rather than exact share. <em>Precise brand share: Not publicly verified.</em>
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Climate &amp; Duty Demands</h2>
    <p>
      Africa's operating conditions stress a battery in three ways at once. Heat is the first: the
      Sahara and Sahel run a hot desert climate (K&ouml;ppen BWh) with mean temperatures sometimes over
      30&deg;C and summer highs over 40&deg;C, reaching 47&deg;C in places
      (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sahara_desert_(ecoregion)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia — Sahara ecoregion</a>;
      <a href="https://www.britannica.com/place/Sahara-desert-Africa/Climate" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Britannica — Sahara climate</a>).
      High ambient temperature accelerates plate ageing and increases electrolyte water loss in
      flooded designs. Dust is the second: unpaved and semi-paved roads raise dust that can bridge
      terminals and drain current. Vibration is the third: rough terrain transmits constant shock to
      the battery tray.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical answer for buyers is a thick-plate (Pb-Sb) flooded construction with secure
      hold-downs and disciplined electrolyte care, or a sealed AGM option where a fleet prefers
      maintenance-free operation that keeps dust and water loss out of the equation. Higher-altitude
      East African markets (for example Nairobi, with a mean of about 18.8&deg;C) are gentler on
      batteries than the Sahel and coastal West Africa
      (<a href="https://en.climate-data.org/africa/kenya/nairobi/nairobi-541/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Climate-Data.org — Nairobi</a>).
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Vehicle Mix &amp; Common Battery Groups</h2>
    <p>
      The battery group follows the vehicle. Japanese trucks — Hino 500/700, Isuzu F/Giga, Mitsubishi
      Fuso Fighter/Super Great and UD Quon — are specified in JIS <strong>N150</strong> (and N200 where
      heavier cranking or a taller case is required). Chinese trucks (Sinotruk HOWO, FAW, Shacman,
      Foton) are also commonly fitted in the N150/N200 group by local aftermarket references.
      European vehicles in North Africa and South Africa (Mercedes-Benz Actros, Volvo FH, Scania,
      MAN) take DIN/EN batteries in codes DIN88 and DIN100, with BCI Group 31 present in some South
      African mining fleets. The groups that dominate African commercial fleets are:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Battery group</th><th>DINWEYS model</th><th>Capacity / CCA</th><th>Typical vehicle class</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>JIS N150</td><td>145G51</td><td>135 Ah · 900 A (JIS)</td><td>Japanese, Korean &amp; Chinese heavy trucks, buses</td></tr>
        <tr><td>JIS N200</td><td>190H52</td><td>200 Ah · 1100 A (JIS)</td><td>Heavy trucks, semi-trailers, high-load buses</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DIN88</td><td>58827</td><td>88 Ah · 800 A (EN)</td><td>European trucks (MAN, Scania, Volvo, Mercedes)</td></tr>
