      Group-to-vehicle mapping is a third-party fitment reference (Club Assist / Century selection
      guides) — a compatible reference, not a DINWEYS OEM confirmation. Confirm dimensions, terminal
      layout and voltage against the specific truck before ordering.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Standards</h2>
    <p>
      Two standards run side by side. JIS N-series batteries (N150/N200) serve the Japanese, Korean
      and Chinese truck base across Sub-Saharan Africa — the workhorse of the region's fleets.
      DIN/EN batteries in codes DIN88 and DIN100 cover European vehicles in North Africa and South
      Africa, with BCI Group 31 appearing in some South African mining applications. Match the
      battery to the vehicle's group, dimensions and terminal orientation rather than to the region
      alone. For a technical comparison of the standards, see
      <a href="/compare/jis-vs-din-vs-bci/" style="color:#1e5aa8;">JIS vs DIN vs BCI</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">DINWEYS Relevance</h2>
    <p>
      DINWEYS — manufactured by Chengguang Power Tech Co., Ltd. — carries the two group families that
      dominate African fleets in one line: JIS N150 (145G51) and N200 (190H52) for the Japanese,
      Korean and Chinese truck base, and DIN88 (58827) and DIN100 (60038) for European vehicles in
      North Africa and South Africa. These are positioned as <strong>compatible references</strong>
      matched by group, dimensions and CCA — not as OEM replacements — so a distributor can cover a
      mixed fleet from a single manufacturer and a single shipment. BCI Group 31 is available on
      request for fleets that specify it.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Buyer Considerations</h2>
    <ol class="takeaways-list">
      <li><strong>Match the battery to the vehicle, not the country.</strong> Confirm group size, physical dimensions, terminal orientation and system voltage (many heavy trucks are 24V — two 12V batteries in series) against the specific unit.</li>
      <li><strong>Budget for heat, dust and vibration together.</strong> High ambient temperatures shorten service life, dust can bridge terminals, and rough roads transmit shock. A thick-plate flooded design or sealed AGM, with secure hold-downs and disciplined electrolyte care, is worth more here than in temperate markets.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear conformity before shipment.</strong> Schemes vary by country — SONCAP (Nigeria), KEBS PVoC (Kenya) and SABS/NRCS (South Africa) are examples of pre-shipment verification of conformity. Confirm the current scheme and labelling with your importer.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan the port.</strong> Durban (South Africa) and Mombasa (Kenya) serve the east and south; Lagos Apapa/Tin Can (Nigeria), Tema (Ghana), Abidjan (C&ocirc;te d'Ivoire) and Dakar (Senegal) serve West Africa; Tanger Med (Morocco) is a major transshipment hub. Choose the entry point nearest your distribution corridor.</li>
    </ol>

